\section*{Field of the Invention} Agricultural microbiology; integrated pond, bog water treatment, microbial cultivation, and sump-to-soil irrigation systems that generate compost tea byproduct while conditioning irrigation water with beneficial microbial consortia.
\section*{Background} Conventional farm ponds and bog filters prioritize water clarity but are underutilized as tunable bioreactors for microbial cultivation. Compost tea production is typically disconnected from irrigation systems, requiring separate equipment and processes. This results in inefficiencies, such as increased labor, higher costs, and missed opportunities to leverage runoff for nutrient cycling. There is a critical need for a closed-loop system that recirculates runoff, cultivates beneficial microbes in situ, and delivers inoculated irrigation water with optional compost tea concentrate, enhancing soil health and crop resilience while reducing chemical inputs.
\section*{Summary of the Invention} The MSSC directs pond water and runoff through a staged bog reactor and sump with controlled aeration and nutrient dosing to cultivate targeted microbial communities. The system conditions a portion of the recirculated flow for irrigation (e.g., drip or spray) and diverts a bleed stream to capture compost tea concentrate. Sensors monitoring dissolved oxygen (DO), oxidation-reduction potential (ORP), pH, electrical conductivity (EC), and temperature, combined with optional AI-driven controls, optimize operating conditions across crop phenological stages.
\section*{Representative Embodiments} A representative installation comprises: (i) a $\sim$60-ft diameter pond; (ii) a 10$\times$40-ft bog filter with graded media and plantings; (iii) a sump with a recirculation pump, aeration manifold, and carbon/nitrogen (C/N) dosing ports; (iv) a tee directing flow to irrigation infrastructure and a bleed line to a compost tea storage tank.
\section*{Claims (Illustrative)} \begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.] \item A sump-centered microbial cultivation system comprising a pond, a planted biofilter, and a sump configured to recirculate water under controlled aeration and nutrient dosing, wherein the system outputs irrigation water inoculated with beneficial microbial consortia. \item The system of claim 1, wherein a bleed stream is diverted to a vessel to produce compost tea concentrate as a byproduct of recirculation. \item The system of claim 1, wherein the planted biofilter includes staged gravel and media beds designed to create redox-zoned microbial habitats. \item The system of claim 1, further comprising sensors to measure at least one of dissolved oxygen, oxidation-reduction potential, electrical conductivity, pH, and temperature, and a controller configured to dynamically adjust aeration and nutrient dosing. \item The system of claim 1, wherein rotational waterfowl grazing primes field rows prior to planting, followed by application of inoculated irrigation water to enhance soil microbial activity. \item The system of claim 1, wherein multi-species manures and plant residues are integrated into the bioreactor under conditions that mitigate pathogen risk while promoting nutrient cycling. \item The system of claim 1, wherein AI-assisted controls dynamically adjust microbial cultivation targets based on crop phenological stages to optimize irrigation output. \end{enumerate}
